{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210735,"nonce":"/YqSGaLE/A/uHK2zsE8I2nmDj/s=","title":"Interpreter set_reg wrote into fiber slots without a write barrier","message":"Fixed; see git log for the landing commit. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n\n`hatch/examples/game/nature-garden` 2026-06-05: load ~20 GLTF scenes into `_scenes`, scatter into a List\u003cMat4\u003e of cached transforms, draw 64 props per frame, then orbit the camera for ~10 seconds. The hatch process SIGSEGVs with `EXC_BAD_ACCESS at 0x0000000000000004` in `runtime::gc::trace_object + 44`, called from `collect_minor + 272` (the remembered-set iteration). The `far` register is 0x4 — meaning the dereferenced ObjHeader pointer is 0x4 (low integer, not a real heap address).\n\n\nRan with `WLIFT_VALIDATE_BARRIERS=1` and got the smoking gun immediately:\n\n```\nthread 'main' panicked at src/runtime/gc.rs:821:21:\nWRITE BARRIER BUG: old 0xb3a8173a0 (Fiber) → young 0x5b2426df8,\nnot in remembered set. desc: fiber.frame[1].values[10]\n```\n\nAn old-gen Fiber holds a pointer to a young object in `frame[1].values[10]`, but the Fiber isn't in `remembered_set`. Without that membership, `collect_minor` doesn't trace the Fiber, doesn't mark the young object, and `process_nursery` drops it. The fiber's slot then dangles. On the next access (during draw, or during the next minor's `process_gray_stack`), the dangling pointer surfaces as a fault.\n\n\n`set_reg` ([src/runtime/vm_interp.rs:688](src/runtime/vm_interp.rs#L688)) is the inner-loop register store:\n\n```rust\nfn set_reg(values: \u0026mut [Value], idx: u16, val: Value) {\n    unsafe { *values.get_unchecked_mut(idx as usize) = val; }\n}\n```\n\nIt doesn't (and shouldn't, for hot-path reasons) emit `write_barrier`. The AOT codegen path emits `wren_write_barrier` at every cross-generation store and is fine; the interpreter has no equivalent. Long-lived suspended fibers — `Fiber.try`/`Fiber.yield` parked fibers, plus the active runner once it ages out of the nursery — accumulate stale young pointers in their stack frames as soon as they receive a young value via `set_reg`.\n\n`hatch/examples/game/grass-field` doesn't hit this because it only allocates two long-lived materials and a tiny instance buffer; nothing churns the nursery hard enough between minors to expose the missing barrier. `procedural-world` doesn't hit it because its foliage goes through `drawMeshInstanced` (no per-prop draw, much less Wren-side allocation per frame).\n\n\n`collect_minor` now walks the `old_objects` linked list once and calls `trace_object` on every `ObjType::Fiber`. That's equivalent to \"every old fiber is implicitly always in remembered_set\" without the bookkeeping overhead of pushing on every promotion + rebuilding the set after each minor.\n\nPatch in [src/runtime/gc.rs](src/runtime/gc.rs#L1235), inserted right after the remembered_set sweep:\n\n```rust\nunsafe {\n    let mut current = self.old_objects;\n    while !current.is_null() {\n        if (*current).obj_type == ObjType::Fiber {\n            trace_object(current, \u0026mut gray_stack);\n        }\n        current = (*current).next;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCost is `O(#old objects)` per minor — old_objects is a singly-linked list, so walking it is one pointer chase per entry. Tracing each fiber is what `remembered_set` would have caused anyway if `set_reg` had emitted a barrier on the offending store; we just always do it for every old fiber instead of trying to track which ones need it.\n\n\n- `cargo test --release --lib`: 916/916 pass.\n- `nature-garden` runs 15s headless with no crash; orbit works.\n- `grass-field` and `procedural-world` patterns unaffected.\n- `WLIFT_VALIDATE_BARRIERS=1` still panics on this scenario by design — the validator enforces the barrier-contract direction, not whether the GC traces fibers as roots. That validator becomes a strict \"AOT codegen correctness check\" rather than a \"minor-GC soundness check\" after this fix.\n\n\n- `set_reg` is one of the hottest functions in the interp; adding a barrier per call would be a measurable regression. The team accepted \"no per-store barrier in interp\" without realising fibers can stash young pointers reachable only via their own stack.\n- The barrier-direction validator (`validate_write_barriers`) was written to catch AOT-codegen omissions. It assumed the interp's storefront went through some other narrower channel (closure-upvalue set, class-method install, fiber-error write) where barriers were emitted explicitly — and indeed those *are* covered. The fiber-stack channel is the gap.\n- `grass-field` was the existing canary demo and doesn't push enough Wren objects through `set_reg` per frame to expose it. `procedural-world` uses instanced rendering so it doesn't either. `nature-garden`'s 64 individual draws + 20 GLTF Material caches + cached Mat4 List was the first scene to hammer the gap reliably.\n\n\n- [[project-aot-gc-stack-map-gap]] — same shape but for AOT stack frames; predecessors of this fix.\n- [[project-aot-gc-pass1-covered-fps]] — the AOT side of the GC walker.\n- [[feedback-check-plugin-dylib-age]] — separate red herring during this investigation.\n- [[project-per-draw-ubo-corruption]] — different GC-corruption symptom we fixed earlier in the same session at the gpu-plugin layer.","files":null}]}